# SECTION 5 — ENTERPRISE-WIDE RECORD LIFECYCLE MANAGEMENT

Implement one shared lifecycle architecture with module-specific business validation.

Supported lifecycle states:

- ACTIVE
- INACTIVE / DEACTIVATED
- REMOVED_FROM_LIST
- SOFT_DELETED
- ARCHIVED
- PERMANENTLY_DELETED

These states must have distinct behavior.

## A. ARCHIVE

When archived:

- Persist ARCHIVED state.
- Remove from normal operational lists.
- Preserve original record ID.
- Preserve relationships and historical information.
- Make available in the corresponding dedicated Archive Inbox.
- Make available in Central Archive Management.
- Record actor, timestamp, reason, and retention metadata.
- Start a 30-calendar-day retention period.

## B. REMOVE FROM LIST

When removed:

- Hide from the applicable normal list.
- Preserve the original database record.
- Do not classify it as ARCHIVED.
- Do not classify it as SOFT_DELETED.
- Do not permanently delete the record.
- Provide an authorized restore-to-list workflow.

Determine whether removal is user-specific or company-wide based on the existing business context. Document and enforce the chosen scope consistently.

## C. SOFT DELETE

When soft deleted:

- Persist SOFT_DELETED.
- Preserve the record.
- Display the status through the appropriate list filter.
- Prevent ordinary operational use.
- Provide authorized restoration.
- Preserve historical references.

## D. DEACTIVATE

When deactivated:

- Persist INACTIVE or DEACTIVATED.
- Keep visible in normal lists.
- Sort below active records.
- Exclude from new operational transactions.
- Preserve historical references.
- Provide Reactivate.

## E. PERMANENT DELETE

Allow only when the record has no protected dependencies and applicable retention requirements permit deletion.

Use backend dependency checks and transactional deletion.

Never destroy posted journal entries, statutory history, protected audit records, or required financial evidence.

